.TH  narrowsh  1 "2026/10/31"

.SH narrowsh - another restricted shell

.PP
narrowsh is a very, VERY, basic shell created to handle situations where an untrusted party, and particularly an automated process, is given access to a system via telnet, ssh or the like. 
.PP
By default it will only accept commands using the \(aq-c\(aq shell option (which is how ssh runs commands), however it can be configured to read commands line-by-line from stdin. It was specifically created for use with ppp-over-ssh vpns that launch pppd via ssh\(aqs "-c" command-line-option, but shouldn\(aqt allow any other commands to be run from ssh. 
.PP
narrowsh is expected to be configured by an administrator/superuser (usually \(aqroot\(aq) and therefore expects configuration files to be in /etc/narrowsh/ and nowhere else. Further it will not run commands if those configuration files are world-writable, or if they are owned by the user that is trying to run narrowsh.
.PP
narrowsh is very basic, and so far supports no shell features like setting environment variables, creating pipelines of commands, or variable subsitution. It exists purely as a means to allow remote entities to run selected commands, and only those commands, on a system. 
.PP
narrowsh can be configured on a user-by-user basis to only allow certain commands to be run, and on both a user and command basis it can apply a number of security limits or features to the session. 
.PP
These features and limits are:
.PP
.in +2
b) Disable use of shell primatives that allow running other processes.
.br
c) Disable \(aqsu\(aq, \(aqsudo\(aq or priviledge escalation by \(aqsuid\(aq methods (prctrl(PR_NO_NEW_PRIVS)).
.br
d) No access to network (using \(aqunshare\(aq to disconnect from network namespace)
.br
e) No access to IPC (using \(aqunshare\(aq to disconnect from IPC namespace)
.br
f) \(aqfake\(aq hostname support (using UTC namespace)
.br
g) Configurable inactivity timeout
.br
h) Secondary Challenge-Response authentication support
.br
i) Google-authenticator compatible OTP support
.br
j) Attempt to prevent further execs by using resource limits to block fork()
.br
k) Basic menu system
.br
.in

.PP
unfortunately some of thesee features, those that use namespaces or the \(aqdisable suid\(aq feature, are linux specific.
.SH THE USUAL CAVEATS

.PP
Restricted shells suffer from one significant failing: if access is granted to any application that allows the user to launch other applications, then their security is undermined. There\(aqs a number of methods that could be used to fix this, but in the initial release of narrowsh the only one used is the \(aquser maximum processes\(aq resource limit. If narrowsh is run with \(aq-X\(aq or \(aqnoexec\(aq options, then just before an application is run, the number of processes rlimit (RLIMIT_NPROC) is set to \(aq1\(aq. This doesn\(aqt prevent execs, but it does prevent forks, and normally commands that can launch other programs don\(aqt replace themselves with those other programs, but fork first and then the child process switches to the desired program using exec. Thus preventing fork should prevent running further programs, and has been seen to do so. However, there may be ways to get around this methodology.
.PP
In future it is expected that other methods will be added to combat \(aqlaunching an app from an app\(aq.
.SH USAGE

.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

    narrowsh
    narrowsh <options> <command line>
.RE
.EE
.fi
.in
.ad b
.nop  
.PP
without any arguments narrowsh tries to open /etc/narrowsh/<username>.conf and read settings from that
